  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nForecast focus remains on the evolution of a longwave trough early \nnext week into Wednesday. A synoptically driven mid-latitude system \nis progged with a Colorado low deepening as it tracks into the Great \nPlains on Monday. Impressive moisture transport ahead of this \nsystem is expected with PW values around 1.5\" or near the 99th \npercentile and a daily max for Nov 2nd. NBM precip chances ramp\nup Monday PM into Tuesday (50-90%) as the upper low pushes out \nof the Southern Rockies into the Central Plains. Well above \nnormal highs in the mid 60s/low 70s are forecast for \nMonday/Tuesday, which is still 5-10 degrees below records. It \nwill also be breezy with southeast to south winds gusting 20-30 \nmph. For rainfall potential, the NBM 72-hr exceedance \nprobabilities of 1\" ending 12z Thursday remain around 50-80% for\nmost locations. \n\nWednesday onward, behind the main synoptic system there will be \nperiodic lower end chances for rain, but amounts should be \nunder a quarter inch. Temperatures will be slightly cooler with\nhighs in the 60s and lows in the 40s, albeit still above normal\nfor early November.",
